- op: discuss  # 26bd
  at: '2026-08-02T21:54:55Z'  # 26bd
  by: agent  # 26bd
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 26bd
  clock: 3  # 26bd
  nonce: 26bd  # 26bd
  message: "FIXED and merged, PR #171, dev 3038ff6707.\n\nROOT CAUSE. _match_propagation_entry short-circuited every RESOLVED edge to an\nungated `return hits[0]`, so a first-party symbol matched a catalog primitive BY\nNAME ALONE. The justifying comment said \"the symbol is already disambiguated by\nresolution\" — false. Resolution establishes WHICH IN-REPO SYMBOL is called, not\nwhether it IS the catalogued external primitive. Third L50 instance of the day:\na boundary-justifying comment stating a false fact and foreclosing the option it\ndeclares unnecessary.\n\nFIX. The entry's declared module is compared against the symbol's own PATH,\nnormalised to module shape, using the component-aware predicate. The SUFFIX arm\nadded by WI-zazul is what makes it work: hypergumbo_core.cli matches a path of\npackages/.../hypergumbo_core/cli.py while log/slog does not match logging.go. A\ntest pins the first-party-catalog case so this cannot decay into \"reject\neverything resolved\" — which would break every user-supplied catalog and is the\ncase the original comment was protecting.\n\nCOHORT EVIDENCE (same 9 repos, same checkouts, same claims YAML, instrument\ncheck on each arm asserting the gate is active before measuring):\n    non-realizable  43 -> 13\n    flows          312 -> 272\nThe 13 survivors are ALL net/http+Do, where the match is CORRECT and only the\nlabel is lossy (emitted module = package, catalog = net/http.Client).\n\nALL 23 REMOVED FLOWS VERIFIED INDIVIDUALLY AGAINST SOURCE, not counted:\n  14  first-party sinks. caddy's `func Log() *zap.Logger` RETURNS a logger and\n      writes nothing (18 rows); d3's `function log()` builds d3.scaleLog — THE\n      LOGARITHM, INV-karud's headline example, which survived WI-zazul because\n      it was never a substring defect.\n   6  caddy's own Run(cfg) matched to gin.Engine.Run — a framework caddy does\n      not use. The SINK was real; the SOURCE was fabricated. Counting would\n      have missed this entirely; only reading each removed flow found it.\n   3  real flows through a first-party THIN WRAPPER\n      (def run(cmd): return subprocess.run(...).stdout). Filed WI-sirod. The fix\n      belongs in propagation. Restoring 3 real flows by readmitting 20 false\n      ones is a bad trade.\n\nPROCESS NOTE WORTH KEEPING. A length heuristic for \"file extension\" (\"a short\nalphanumeric segment after a dot\") rewrote the real module net.ws to net and was\ncaught by a PRE-EXISTING test within seconds. No threshold separates an\nextension from a short module component; the set is now named explicitly and\npinned by tests. That was the fourth time in this arc that a pre-existing test\ncaught a design error of mine — the others being the Go slash-vs-dot assumption\nand the unqualified-System suffix case. Running the whole affected suite rather\nthan only the new class paid for itself every time.\n\nRESIDUAL, tracked not hidden: WI-sirod (thin-wrapper propagation), WI-nibav\n(defusedcsv catalog gap), and the 13 net/http+Do labelling flows which need\nadjudication — correct match, unverifiable by lookup."  # 26bd
